1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
When you ship a cloud application, you often hit a gap.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
The model's built-in safety isn't the same as your product's content policy,

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
and no single prompt line enforces the rules your deployment actually needs.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
This lesson covers content policy and how builders layer guardrails to enforce it.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Let's break it down.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Let's get three building blocks straight because the rest of the lesson leans on them.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
First, your content policy.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
That's the set of rules your application defines for what it will and won't produce or act on.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
It's enforced through the model's own safety, plus your own guardrails.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Second, guardrail layering.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
You place controls at multiple points.

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Screening on input, checks on output, and enforcement in your code,

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
so that no single failure exposes the system.

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Third, defense in depth.

15
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
That's the security principle behind the layering.

16
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,000
You stack overlapping controls, so when one is bypassed, the next one catches it.

17
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Content policy is the what. Layering is the how.

18
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Defense in depth is why it holds.

19
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Now, here's a distinction that trips up a lot of new builders.

20
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
The model's built-in safety isn't the same thing as your content policy.

21
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Cloud ships with real safety behavior.

22
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
It's trained to decline requests that are broadly harmful, and that protection is genuine.

23
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
But Cloud can't know the bespoke rules of your specific deployment.

24
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Maybe your app should never give medical dosing advice,

25
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
or never touch a customer's payment details, or never discuss a competitor.

26
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
That's an application-specific rule, and only you can define it.

27
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
The model's safety is a floor you build on.

28
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Enforcing what this particular product refuses is the developer's own guardrail job,

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
and that's the work this lesson is about.

30
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
So why not just write one strong rule into your system prompt and call it done?

31
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Because a single prompt-level guardrail is probabilistic.

32
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Its guidance and guidance can be talked around.

33
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
A determined user can rephrase their way past it,

34
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
and a piece of injected content can override it entirely.

35
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,000
The model usually follows the instruction, but usually isn't the same as always.

36
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
For a low-stakes preference, that might be fine.

37
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
For a high-stakes constraint, something where one failure

38
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
means real harm or a compliance breach, probabilistic isn't good enough.

39
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,000
That's the whole reason we layer.

40
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
One instruction is a reasonable starting point,

41
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
but it should never be the entire defense for anything that actually matters.

42
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Let's name the three layers you'll actually build.

43
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
The first is input screening.

44
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Before content ever reaches the model, you screen or delimit it,

45
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
catching hostile input early.

46
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Whether it came from the user or from a tool result.

47
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
The second is output checks.

48
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
After the model responds, you validate that response before it's released to the user

49
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
or handed to a downstream tool, so an unsafe answer gets blocked before it does anything.

50
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
The third is code-level enforcement.

51
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
This is the layer people forget.

52
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,000
You enforce policy directly in your own application code,

53
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,000
so a rule still holds even when the model is bypassed.

54
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Input screening, output checks, code-level enforcement.

55
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Those three are your defense in depth.

56
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Let's zoom in on the input side.

57
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Input guardrails act on content before the model ever sees it,

58
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
and that early position is exactly what makes them powerful.

59
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
The simplest version is screening.

60
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
You run incoming user input through a lightweight classifier

61
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
that looks for hostile patterns, known jailbreak phrasing,

62
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
obvious injection attempts, and flag anything suspicious.

63
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
The second move is delimiting.

64
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
When content is untrusted, you wrap it in clear boundaries

65
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,000
so the model reads it as data, not as instructions to follow.

66
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:05,000
And when something fails a check, you reject it or sanitize it before it goes any further.

67
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,000
The point is simple.

68
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,000
If bad input never reaches the model, the model never gets a chance to act on it.

69
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Now the output side, and there are three checks worth building.

70
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
First, validate before release.

71
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Before a response reaches the user,

72
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
you check it against your policy and block anything disallowed.

73
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
This is your last line before the content leaves your system.

74
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Second, screen before downstream tools.

75
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,000
In an agent, a response often triggers a tool call or a real action,

76
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
so you validate the output before it fires,

77
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
because an unsafe response that moves money or deletes a file does real damage.

78
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Third, monitor continuously.

79
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,000
You log and review outputs over time, looking for signs of a successful attack,

80
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
and you feed what you learn back into your prompts and filters.

81
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Validate, screen before acting, and monitor.

82
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Three habits that keep the output side honest.

83
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Now let's talk placement.

84
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
In the jailbreak lesson, you saw the enterprise stack,

85
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,000
a hardened system prompt, a haiku class harmlessness screen,

86
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
on input, and screening on tool output.

87
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
That same stack is what policy enforcement placement looks like in practice,

88
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
so keep it as your reference build.

89
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Here's what happens when one of those layers is missing.

90
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Picture a health information app that shipped the hardened prompt and the input screen,

91
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
but skipped the screening on tool output.

92
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Then a retrieved document carries an injected instruction,

93
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
and because nothing checks what the tool hands back,

94
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
the model relays specific dosing advice the content policy forbids.

95
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:47,000
The prompt held, the input screen held.

96
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
The incident walked through the one layer that wasn't there.

97
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
That's the postmortem lesson.

98
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Every skipped enforcement point is where a failure will eventually walk through.

99
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Let's be honest about the trade-offs, because layering isn't free.

100
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,000
On the benefit side, layered input and output classifiers

101
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
filter the large majority of jailbreak attempts.

102
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Anthropic's own research shows this kind of classifier layering

103
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
sharply reduces successful jailbreaks,

104
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,000
and it gives you a defense-in-depth posture that no single control can match on its own.

105
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
That's real, and it's worth building.

106
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:25,000
But there's a cost.

107
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Classifiers add some compute overhead,

108
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
since you're running extra checks on every request.

109
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
They also raise your over-refusal rate a little,

110
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
meaning a few legitimate requests get caught in the net,

111
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:41,000
and they still won't stop every possible jailbreak.

112
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
So layering isn't about reaching perfect.

113
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
It's about stacking enough good enough layers

114
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
that getting through all of them at once is very hard.

115
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Let's frame the big picture.

116
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Model safety gives you a floor, but it isn't your whole content policy.

117
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Your rules need their own layered guardrails.

118
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Screening input, checking output, and enforcing in code.

119
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
Just remember, one prompt line is probabilistic.

120
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:10,000
Never the whole defense.

121
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,000
That covers layering input and output guardrails.

122
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
When you're ready, I'll see you in the next one.

